9. Is there a Spring annotation for tenant-specific execution?

→ There is no standard Spring annotation that automatically guarantees one execution per tenant.
→ I can create a custom annotation such as @OncePerTenant and implement it using Spring AOP.
→ The aspect can validate the tenant and call a database-backed guard.
→ The database unique constraint is essential because an annotation or in-memory flag alone cannot prevent concurrent execution across multiple application instances.

